<!--
    Objective-C selector stubs typed only from agreeing declarations
    (`option objcstubagree on|off`, with `option objc on`).

    Issue: one `objc_msgSend$<sel>` stub serves every receiver of its
    selector, but it took its prototype from whatever declaration the image
    held. A protocol declaring `- (double)length` typed `objc_msgSend$length`
    as returning a double for every sender, so after `[name length]` on a
    string (an integer in x0) x0 read as dead and the next call lost every
    argument: `objc_msgSend$length(arg3); if (sub_103434eb4())` where the
    untyped image reads `sub_103434eb4(v2,a7,1,"Empty table name...")`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 external-refinement.
    `analyzers/objc/kuna_objcrefs.rs` `Selectors::stub_encoding` types a stub
    from the image's agreed spelling only when the SDK headers (`objcsdk`),
    when they spell the selector, agree on the register classes of its return
    and arguments, and a floating-point return only a protocol declares (no
    class or category of the image implements it) needs that confirmation.

    P0 assertions: `option objc on`, `option objcstubagree on|off`,
    `option objcsdk <directory>|off`.

    The fixture is `macho_objc_stubagree.m`: protocol `Measured` declares
    `-(double)length` with no implementer, class `Ruler` implements
    `-(double)tag`, and `validate` sends `length` and `tag` to a `Str` (whose
    methods return integers) through stubs, each result passed to `check` with
    three more arguments.

    Pass 1 (`objcstubagree off`) reproduces the BUG: `objc_msgSend$length(a0);`
    then `if (check())`.
    Pass 2 (default) is the FIX for the protocol-only selector:
    `check(v1,a2,1,"empty name")`; `tag`, implemented here as a double and
    unknown without headers, still loses `check`'s arguments.
    Pass 3 (default + `objcsdk` on the hand-written `objc_sdk/Widget.h`, which
    declares an NSInteger `tag`) drops the disagreeing `tag` prototype too:
    `check(objc_msgSend$tag(a1),a2,2,"bad tag")`.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c0035fd6
</bytechunk>
